# The installer writes .env for you (see docs/install.md); this is a reference for every
# setting it can hold (docs/configuration.md explains them).
#
# API keys (TMDB, OMDb, Hardcover…) are entered in the app — the first-run setup, or
# Settings → Connections — not here. These three lines are only a fallback older installs
# used; leave them empty.
ARRMADA_TMDB_API_KEY=
ARRMADA_OMDB_API_KEY=
ARRMADA_HARDCOVER_API_KEY=

# What updates bring: stable = the published releases, downloaded (the default); main = the
# newest code on main, compiled on this server on every update (for developers).
ARRMADA_CHANNEL=stable

# The published image update.sh downloads on the stable channel (this one when unset).
# ARRMADA_IMAGE=ghcr.io/tristenlammi/arrmada:stable

# The image docker compose runs. arrmada:dev is update.sh's own build or download (the
# installer and update.sh write this line); without it, a plain `docker compose up -d`
# runs the published image. See docs/updating.md.
ARRMADA_APP_IMAGE=arrmada:dev

# Where the database and settings live on the host, and the user Arrmada runs as
# (99 / 100 on Unraid).
# ARRMADA_DATA_HOST=/mnt/user/appdata/arrmada/data
# ARRMADA_PUID=1000
# ARRMADA_PGID=1000

# Database: SQLite stays the default. PostgreSQL 16–18 is opt-in and requires an
# existing UTF8 database and a dedicated schema owned by the application role.
# Startup creates a missing schema if the role has CREATE on the database;
# restricted roles can use an operator-provisioned schema instead.
# Conversion is offline and explicit; changing this does not copy SQLite data.
# See docs/postgresql.md. Keep .env mode 0600; URL credentials must be URL-encoded.
ARRMADA_DATABASE_BACKEND=sqlite
# ARRMADA_POSTGRES_URL=postgres://arrmada_app:URL_ENCODED_PASSWORD@db.example/existing_db?sslmode=verify-full&sslrootcert=/certs/ca.pem
# ARRMADA_POSTGRES_SCHEMA=arrmada
# ARRMADA_POSTGRES_MAX_CONNECTIONS=4
# Native clients are bundled in the image. For a bare binary, install clients
# 16–18 no older than the server; optionally select their absolute directory:
# ARRMADA_POSTGRES_BIN=/usr/lib/postgresql/18/bin

# Media. /media inside is Arrmada's own downloads and library: the Docker volume
# arrmada-media unless this names a folder on the host. Arrmada, qBittorrent and SABnzbd all
# mount it at the same path, so imports hardlink. A full path, and never the
# ARRMADA_DATA_HOST folder (or inside it). Not the /storage folder below either: two mounts
# of one folder can't hardlink between them. Run update.sh after changing it.
# ARRMADA_MEDIA_HOST=/mnt/user/arrmada-media
#
# Your own media folder at /storage. With docker-compose.yml the installer mounts it (in
# docker-compose.override.yml; change it with install.sh --media) and writes this line for
# reference. With docker-compose.example.yml (plain Compose, Portainer) this line is the mount.
# ARRMADA_STORAGE_HOST=/mnt/user/data

# Host ports. install.sh auto-picks free ones so nothing clashes with Radarr (7878),
# an existing qBittorrent (8080), etc. The app always listens on 7878 *inside* the
# container; ARRMADA_PORT is only the host port it's published on.
ARRMADA_PORT=7878
ARRMADA_QBIT_WEBUI_PORT=8080

# Incoming BitTorrent port. Pick a random high port (the install script does this
# automatically). Arrmada publishes it in Docker AND applies it inside qBittorrent,
# so the two always match — you just forward this port on your router (TCP + UDP).
ARRMADA_QBIT_PORT=6881

# Library folders. By default every module scans one shared library (/media/library inside
# the container); the folders picked in the app (Settings → Library) win over these. To point Movies / TV / Books at SEPARATE folders, set these — each falls back
# to the shared library when unset. EBOOKS and AUDIOBOOKS can be the same path (both under one
# /books folder) or different paths (kept apart).
# ARRMADA_MOVIES_DIR=/media/movies
# ARRMADA_TV_DIR=/media/tvshows
# ARRMADA_EBOOKS_DIR=/media/ebooks
# ARRMADA_AUDIOBOOKS_DIR=/media/audiobooks

# Insights (Plex watch monitoring). Point Arrmada at your Plex server. You can also set
# these in the Insights → Settings UI. Token: in Plex web, play an item → ⋯ → Get Info →
# View XML — the URL ends with X-Plex-Token=...
ARRMADA_PLEX_URL=
ARRMADA_PLEX_TOKEN=

# Timezone. Schedules run in this zone — the Convert encode window especially, which
# otherwise means overnight-in-London rather than overnight where you are. install.sh
# detects this from the host; set it yourself if that guessed wrong.
# Full list: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_tz_database_time_zones
TZ=Etc/UTC
